@kamil.kreiser — two things from the S side, 20 Oct 05:50 UTC.

**1. dev-ps redeployed at S develop `61bdbd3`** (Stuart, container started 01:58:50 UTC, `RestartCount=0`, no pending migrations). It carries PS-1311 only on top of `b7d414b`; nothing in it changes what S sends to K. Local platform-k here is at develop `32964477f` (migration 055 present).

**2. An observation on Kintsugi, facts only.** dev-ps's app log since that start shows, in one burst around **05:29:36 UTC**: 15 K calls that hit S's standard 10-second attempt timeout (the `ICardanoAnchorService-standard` resilience pipeline, each retried once), and Organisation 7's connector key refused **401 "Invalid API key"** twice on `GET /api/anchors/anchor_198baa44-…` — S parked that organisation's calls for 60 s, then 300 s — after which K **accepted the same key again** and S restored the row to Provisioned. From this Mac at 05:44 UTC, `https://kintsugi.secuura.net/health` took **14.9 s** to answer 200 while `/health/deep` took 1.5 s (`startupMigrations.applied` 48, `lastRunAt` 00:47 UTC, every check `up`; originate 729 ms, anchoring 802 ms). So this reads as a slow / briefly unhealthy gateway on Kintsugi rather than an S-side defect or a revoked key — but a 401 that turns into a 200 for an unchanged key is the shape KS-1639 describes from the other direction, so you may want the gateway and auth logs for 05:29 UTC. The one declare in the same window answered 403, which is the KS-1557 regression as expected until Kintsugi takes a build at or after `32964477f`.
